diff --git a/docs/extensions.md b/docs/extensions.md
index d815192d299f7992e7fccd89803c341fd3ac74ae..899d045eb4dba345a94dfab4f9931ba6ff6a6b77 100644
--- a/docs/extensions.md
+++ b/docs/extensions.md
@@ -290,6 +290,140 @@ prompt-on-first-load gate (unlike pi). Putting code into `.autosk/extensions/`
 or naming a package in `settings.json` *is* the consent. Treat these locations
 as you would any code you run.
 
+## Runtime identity
+
+Every extension is identified by the exact bytes it was loaded from. Before the
+loader imports an entry it walks the extension's **root** — the package directory,
+the extension subdirectory, or the single file, whichever discovery found — digests
+every regular file, and hashes the sorted listing. The entry's own path is *not*
+part of the digest, so every entry a package declares shares one identity — the
+distribution is the unit, not the module. Enrolling a task records that digest on
+the task, and enroll, resume, dispatch and both transition paths re-check it. If
+the digest no longer matches, the task stops with `extension_version_mismatch`
+instead of continuing on code it was never admitted to; a `work` task is parked
+with that reason.
+
+For a directory extension the root is the discovery unit and never merely the entry
+file's own directory. A package that declares `dist/index.js` would otherwise be
+identified by `dist/` alone, while `dist/index.js` is free to
+`import("../lib/...")` — the ordinary compiled-npm layout, so that mistake would
+defeat the guarantee silently in the common case.
+
+A **bare `foo.ts`** sitting directly in an extensions directory is its own
+distribution: the file, and nothing else. There is no root that is both complete
+and stable for that layout. Rooting it at the shared directory would cover its
+sibling imports, but then adding an unrelated extension — or Finder writing
+`.DS_Store` — would move its identity and park tasks pinned to it, and one
+symlinked neighbour would make it unidentifiable altogether. So what a bare file
+imports — a sibling file, a helper subdirectory — is **outside** its identity,
+exactly as a hoisted npm dependency is. A subdirectory that holds code but declares
+no entry point of its own is reported in `project.diagnostics`, so this layout is
+visible rather than silent, but the diagnostic is a warning and not a guarantee: it
+cannot see a sibling `.ts` file, which discovery loads as its own distribution.
+
+**If you want tree-wide coverage, put the extension in its own subdirectory** (or
+install it as a package). Then the whole subdirectory is the distribution and every
+file it contains is part of the identity.
+
+The identity is bound at the **first import** of an entry and reused for the life
+of the process. That is not an optimisation, it is the only truthful choice: Bun
+keeps serving the module it already imported for a path, so editing an installed
+extension and reloading gives you the *old* code until the process restarts (see
+[The live-code hazard](#the-live-code-hazard)). Taking the digest from disk on a
+rebuild would advertise an identity for code the daemon is not running — and a
+later restart would make that false pin match. A rebuild that finds different
+bytes therefore keeps the identity of what is running and records a
+`project.diagnostics` entry telling the operator a restart is needed.
+
+After the restart the extension is read fresh, its identity is the new one, and a
+task still pinned to the old identity is refused — which is the protection working
+as intended.
+
+The whole directory is covered on purpose. A static import scan cannot see
+computed `import(p)`, `require(p)`, `eval`-based import or real `createRequire`
+targets, so hashing only the entry file would claim more than it proves.
+
+What this is not: it pins bytes on disk. It is not a sandbox, and it says nothing
+about what the code does once imported or what it fetches at runtime.
+
+Nor is it a defence against an actor who can write into `.autosk/`. The pin lives in
+`task.json`, which the store shares with the operator; anyone who can rewrite that
+file can also replace the extension it points at, so forging a digest buys nothing
+that editing the code did not already give.
+
+What the engine does guarantee is that **a missing pin is never quietly filled in**.
+`runtime_identity` is engine-owned and the metadata API refuses to edit it, so
+workflow code cannot clear it through the supported surface; and on a task that
+already carries a workflow, an absent pin is treated exactly like a mismatched one.
+Deleting the reserved key does not release the task — dispatch and both transition
+paths park it, and resuming or re-enrolling it *at the step it sits on* is refused,
+because either would continue mid-flight on code the task was never admitted under.
+
+**One act re-pins, and it is the same act in every case: entering a workflow at its
+first step.** That is a restart — the task begins the workflow again, on today's
+code — so it re-pins whatever it carried before: nothing, the wrong digest, or a
+digest from another workflow. It is how a task parked by an ordinary `ext update`
+becomes runnable again, and how a task enrolled before identities existed picks one
+up. A task that has no workflow yet was never admitted anywhere and pins freely at
+whatever step it starts on.
+
+Restarting is not the same as continuing, and this feature deliberately offers only
+the first. Continuing an in-flight task across an extension update is the migration
+workflow of #10, which does not exist yet; until it does, an update parks the tasks
+it affects and they restart or wait.
+
+A symbolic link resolving **inside** the root is recorded by its target and not
+followed: the bytes it points at are already covered by their own entry, and
+re-pointing the link changes the digest. A link resolving **outside** the root is
+refused.
+
+Hashing an escaping link's target instead of refusing it was tried and is unsound.
+The runtime resolves a linked module to its realpath, so that module's own relative
+imports load from outside the root — files the digest never sees. The recorded
+bytes would match while the executed code differed.
+
+The escape test resolves each target with `realpath`. A lexical test is not
+enough — `link -> d/up/../..` with `d/up -> ..` normalizes to `.` yet resolves
+above the root.
+
+### What this does not cover — read this before relying on it
+
+The identity covers the distribution's **own tree**. Code the extension resolves
+**outside** that tree is not covered. Concretely:
+
+- An npm dependency **hoisted** into a parent `node_modules` is outside the
+  package root and needs no symlink at all. Updating that dependency under a
+  running Epic does not move the digest.
+- A **pnpm-style** layout, whose `node_modules/` is filled with links to sibling
+  stores, is refused outright with a `project.diagnostics` entry rather than
+  identified as if the dependency were absent.
+
+So "a task runs only on the extension bytes it was enrolled on" means the bytes of
+the extension's own distribution. It pins what an `ext update` to the extension
+itself changes — the realistic update path — and it does not pin the dependency
+graph around it. Extending the identity to the resolved dependency graph is
+separate work.
+
+Three more consequences worth knowing:
+
+- A distribution the loader cannot identify is **refused**, not loaded
+  unidentified. An escaping directory link, a non-regular file, or a tree beyond
+  the bounds (20 000 files, 5 000 directories, 256 MiB) each produce a
+  `project.diagnostics` entry and no registration. Each bound is checked before
+  the work it guards, so a hostile tree cannot burn unbounded time first.
+- A task enrolled before this existed carries no pin. On a task that already has
+  a workflow an absent pin is refused exactly like a mismatched one, so it parks
+  once and is re-enrolled at the workflow's first step; nothing adopts a missing
+  pin on an automatic path. The transition check runs *before* the session claims
+  its settle, so a registry swapped mid-session parks the task and ends the
+  session rather than stranding both. A pin that has been hand-edited into an invalid shape
+  is refused outright and never repaired — including when the task is being
+  switched to another workflow, so a refusal cannot be washed out by enrolling
+  away and back.
+- Filenames are digested as raw bytes, not as decoded strings, so two distinct
+  names that are not valid UTF-8 cannot collapse onto the same digest. (APFS
+  refuses such names outright; the case is reachable on Linux.)
+
 ## Error isolation & `project.diagnostics`
 
 A broken extension never takes the daemon down. Each of these is caught,
